      "location": "BuilderWallet.init",
      "explanation": "BuilderWallet uses a two-step initialization pattern where `builderAdmin` is set via an external `init(address _builderAdmin)` call. This function has no access control (not restricted to the factory) and no “only-once” guard. As a result, any address can call `init()` at any time after deployment to overwrite `builderAdmin` and become the authorized sweeper. Since these wallets are deterministic fee recipients (computed via CREATE2 in RiskEngine), they are expected to accumulate tokens; an attacker can hijack any deployed BuilderWallet and steal all ERC20 balances via `sweep()`.",
      "attack_scenario": "1) Identify a builderCode whose BuilderWallet is deployed and has accumulated fees (tokens).\n2) Call `BuilderWallet(wallet).init(attackerAddress)` from an EOA (no permissions required).\n3) Call `BuilderWallet(wallet).sweep(token, attackerAddress)`.\n4) The wallet transfers its entire balance of `token` to the attacker because `msg.sender` is now `builderAdmin`.",
      "suggested_fix": "Harden initialization:\n- Restrict `init` to the factory: `require(msg.sender == FACTORY, ...)`.\n- Make it single-use: `require(builderAdmin == address(0), \"ALREADY_INIT\")`.\nPreferably set `builderAdmin` in the constructor (include it in initCode) to avoid any external initialization step.\nAlso consider emitting an event on initialization/admin changes."
    }
  ],
  "overall_explanation": "The primary exploitable issue is in BuilderWallet: `init()` is publicly callable and can be called repeatedly, allowing anyone to take over `builderAdmin` and sweep all tokens from any deployed builder wallet. This enables direct theft of fee funds sent to these deterministic CREATE2 wallets. No other concrete, non-admin-compromise exploit was identified in the provided RiskEngine logic."
